WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.934
Welcome to Grade 5 English, Unit 7: Tell It Another Way.

2
00:00:04.014 --> 00:00:10.888
Unit 7 explores point of view: who tells a story,
and how that choice changes what a reader sees.

3
00:00:10.968 --> 00:00:15.548
Learners compare two first-person
accounts of one market morning,

4
00:00:15.628 --> 00:00:22.286
then follow a Year 5 class in Ayensu who uncover
a hidden mural and argue about what it means.

5
00:00:22.366 --> 00:00:30.315
Along the way they learn to separate facts from opinions,
to use phrases such as I think that and In my opinion,

6
00:00:30.395 --> 00:00:33.756
and to agree or disagree politely with a reason.

7
00:00:33.836 --> 00:00:40.566
The unit ends with each learner retelling a
familiar story through a brand-new narrator's eyes.

8
00:00:40.646 --> 00:00:46.588
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

9
00:00:46.668 --> 00:00:54.979
By the end of this unit, you will be able to explain what
point of view means and identify the narrator in a text.

10
00:00:55.059 --> 00:01:03.149
You will also compare two versions of the same event
told from different perspectives and explain what changes.

11
00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:10.363
You will also describe how changing the narrator
changes the mood, detail, and feeling of a story.

12
00:01:10.443 --> 00:01:13.086
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

13
00:01:13.166 --> 00:01:18.976
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
local, urban, rural, spacious, continent, colonial.

14
00:01:19.056 --> 00:01:23.894
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

15
00:01:23.974 --> 00:01:27.719
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

16
00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:34.803
The reading sequence includes Two Voices from the Market:
Omar's Account and Two Voices from the Market: Amal's Account.

17
00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:35.629
Read actively.

18
00:01:35.709 --> 00:01:39.702
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

19
00:01:39.782 --> 00:01:43.539
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

20
00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:47.942
The first language focus is Facts
and Opinions: Telling Them Apart.

21
00:01:48.022 --> 00:01:51.030
A fact can be checked and proved with evidence.

22
00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:57.733
An opinion tells us what one person thinks or feels,
so somebody else may reasonably disagree with it.

23
00:01:57.813 --> 00:02:04.895
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

24
00:02:04.975 --> 00:02:08.971
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Hot-Seating a Narrator.

25
00:02:09.051 --> 00:02:13.107
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

26
00:02:13.187 --> 00:02:16.256
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

27
00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:19.529
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

28
00:02:19.609 --> 00:02:21.082
You are ready to begin.

29
00:02:21.162 --> 00:02:27.771
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

30
00:02:27.851 --> 00:02:31.013
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

31
00:02:31.093 --> 00:02:38.512
Use AI English for hints and feedback any time, and bring
your questions to a live session too if you join one.
